#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134922} :activity/id #uuid "9c33b9fa-2311-5a26-bf22-2344e0c3157a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050534 :topic/id #uuid "51703412-3999-597b-8a50-c1bd75195c76" :topic/math-academy-id 2135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135160 :tutorial/content "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." :tutorial/id #uuid "6855af2b-f345-5f4d-aae2-49d6ee758d9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62e98a94-9cd3-5ffc-8b39-fadb81337cfc" :step/math-academy-id 44163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398569}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"} :step/id #uuid "e6742f0e-ef7b-5dd1-89f2-b67bd6968d33" :step/math-academy-id 44752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374193}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"} :step/id #uuid "ce3f9be3-b095-5f66-a582-c68d7cb49d78" :step/math-academy-id 44755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374207}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"} :step/id #uuid "d66524f2-18df-53a2-9007-6aaa99c8c6f4" :step/math-academy-id 44758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135164 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e318cc2d-81e3-5cbd-b0ce-2f076bfd06b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7732 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Flux Across a Surface"} :step/id #uuid "f13db816-3a2c-5bc5-86bb-261c0c695783" :step/math-academy-id 44794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135170 :tutorial/content "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n> *Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n> =\n> \\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n> =\n> \\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca1c4682-71c7-5950-950a-a33ad1e84c23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7773 :tutorial/title "The Divergence Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "91aeb514-1bab-5aae-a601-fdf72405bbbf" :step/math-academy-id 45010 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134936}}] :activity/title "The Divergence Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134012}]}
